          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#interpretation_in_higher_differential_geometry"} Interpretation in higher differential geometry
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-duality
            and in particular of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+T-duality"} differential T-duality
            one considers (as discussed there)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+products"} fiber products
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pairs"} pairs
            of
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus"} torus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundles"} fiber bundles
            equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+2-bundle"} circle 2-bundle
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"}> with connection
            ).
          %p
            In some disguise, this has been called
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_n
              \-geometry
            (
            %a{:href => "#Baraglia"}> Baraglia
            ). The T-duality interpretation is made explicit in
            %a{:href => "Bouwknegt"} Bouwknegt
          %p
            Here “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_n
            ” refers to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+orthogonal+group"} special orthogonal group
            of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SO
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SO(n+1,n)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+tangent+bundle"} generalized tangent bundle
            tensored with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
            (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincare+line+bundle"} Poincare line bundle
            of the T-duality correspondence).
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#interpretation_in_higher_differential_geometry Interpretation in higher differential geometry
          %p
            We give the interpretation of
            = succeed "-geometry" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_n
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoid"} higher differential geometry
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi conn
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %msub
                    %mo ′
                    %mi conn
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_{conn},c'_{conn} \colon X \to \mathbf{B}U(1)
            modulating two circle principal bundles with conection, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+T-duality"} differential T-duality
            structure is a choice of trivialization of their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cup+product"} cup product
            class. From this we get the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting+diagram"} pasting diagram
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullbacks"} homotopy pullbacks
            of smooth
            = succeed "-stacks" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                          %msub
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                          %mover
                            %mi P
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi τ
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mn 2
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi fib
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo ∪
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ×
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mo ∪
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mn 3
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  P \times_X \hat P&amp;\stackrel{\tau}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{B}^2 U(1) &amp;\to&amp; *
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  X
                  \&amp;\to&amp;
                  fib(\cup)
                  \&amp;\to&amp;
                  (\mathbf{B}U(1)) \times (\mathbf{B}U(1))
                  \\\
                  \&amp;\searrow &amp; \downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{\cup}}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; * &amp;\to&amp; \mathbf{B}^3 U(1)
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi τ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
            is the morphism that modulates the circle 2-bundle on the fiber product of the two circle bundles.
          %p (…)
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+T-duality"} differential T-duality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+complex+geometry"} generalized complex geometry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+generalized+geometry"} exceptional generalized geometry
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The term
            = succeed "-geometry" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_n
          %ul
            %li#Baraglia
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Baraglia"}> David Baraglia
              ,
              %em Leibniz algebroids, twistings and exceptional generalized geometry
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1101.0856"}> arXiv:1101.0856
              )
          %p A review is in
          %ul
            %li#Hitchin
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nigel+Hitchin"}> Nigel Hitchin
              , talks at StringMath2012 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://www.hcm.uni-bonn.de/fileadmin/stringmath2012/plenary_talks/03-Hitchin.pdf"}> pdf
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p The relation to T-duality is made clear around slide 80 of:
          %ul
            %li#Bouwknegt
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Bouwknegt"}> Peter Bouwknegt
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Courant Algebroids and Generalizations of Geometry
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.upenn.edu/StringMath2011/notes/Bouwknegt_StringMath2011_talk.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            A discussion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-Lie+theory"} higher Lie theoretic
            aspects is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernesto+Lupercio"}> Ernesto Lupercio
              , Camilo Rengifo,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bernardo+Uribe"} Bernardo Uribe
              %em T-duality and exceptional generalized geometry through symmetries of dg-manifolds
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1208.6048"}> arXiv:1208.6048
              \&amp;rbrack;
